REGIONAL REGULATION OF TASIKMALAYA REGENCY

NUMBER 5 OF 2019

CONCERNING

WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF TASIKMALAYA,

Considering:

a. that every citizen has equal rights to live a dignified life in accordance with the principles of humanity, equality and justice;
b. that women and children are groups of society vulnerable to acts of injustice and inequality as well as acts of violence that can harm their rights and dignity as human beings;
c. that the rampant cases of violence against women and children in Tasikmalaya Regency require handling and concrete action from the Regional Government to eliminate all forms of violence against women and children;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation on Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within West Java Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950), as amended by Law Number 4 of 1968 concerning the Establishment of Purwakarta Regency and Subang Regency by amending Law Number 14 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within West Java Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1968 Number 31, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2851);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
6. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
7. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635) as amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
8. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
9. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
10. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
11. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
12. Government Regulation Number 12 of 2017 concerning Guidance and Supervision of Regional Government Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2017 Number 73, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6041);
13. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of the Republic of Indonesia Number 12 of 2015 concerning Guidelines for Child Participation in Development Planning (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 1754);
14. Regional Regulation of Tasikmalaya Regency Number 7 of 2016 concerning Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus (Regional Gazette of Tasikmalaya Regency of 2016 Number 7 as amended by Regional Regulation of Tasikmalaya Regency Number 1 of 2019 concerning Amendment to Regional Regulation of Tasikmalaya Regency Number 7 of 2016 concerning Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus (Regional Gazette of Tasikmalaya Regency of 2019 Number 1);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF TASIKMALAYA REGENCY

and

REGENT OF TASIKMALAYA

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT AND CHILD PROTECTION.

Article 3

Women's empowerment is directed to obtain opportunities and rights as human beings in order to be able to play a role and participate in the fields of:
a. economy;
b. social culture;
c. politics; and
d. law.

Article 4

The implementation of women's empowerment in the economic field as referred to in Article 3 letter a is carried out through:
a. provision of skills and job training;
b. facilitation of the formation, strengthening and development of productive economic business groups;
c. facilitation and capital assistance;
d. facilitation of marketing network development; and
e. Economic Empowerment of Women Heads of Households (PEKKA).

Article 5

Women's empowerment in the social and cultural field as referred to in Article 3 letter b is carried out through:
a. improvement of knowledge, attitudes and skills to encourage the fulfillment of the right to education in stages according to potential to improve women's social status;
b. improvement of awareness, willingness and ability to overcome health problems through affordable and quality promotive, preventive, curative, and rehabilitative efforts, especially in the field of reproductive health;
c. improvement of awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration; and
d. facilitation and efforts to preserve customs and the development of science, technology, arts and culture for the advancement of women.

Article 6

The implementation of women's empowerment in the political field as referred to in Article 3 letter c includes:
a. political education for women;
b. involvement of women in decision-making at various levels of government;
c. provision of opportunities for women to hold public office;
d. participation in general elections; and
e. self-development through organizations to associate, assemble and express opinions.

Article 10

Child Protection includes the fields of:
a. Child Protection in the field of Religion and Belief;
b. Child Protection in the field of Civil and Freedom;
c. Child Protection in the field of Health;
d. Child Protection in the field of Education;
e. Child Protection in the field of Social; and
f. Child Protection in the field of Special Protection.

Article 12

Child protection in the Civil field as referred to in Article 10 letter b includes:
a. guarantee that every child has the right to obtain a name and self-identity from birth in the form of a birth certificate;
b. the making of birth certificates is the responsibility of the Government;
c. the making of birth certificates is not subject to fees and is borne by the Regional Government; and
d. provisions for making birth certificates are further regulated in Regent Regulation.

Article 13

1. Child protection in the civil and freedom field assists children so that children can:
a. participate in all fields;
b. freely express opinions and think according to conscience and religion in accordance with the norms prevailing in the region;
c. freely receive oral or written information in accordance with the age and development stages of the child;
d. freely associate and assemble in accordance with noble norms and ethics;
e. freely rest, play, recreate, and create arts and culture without neglecting obligations as a child; and
f. obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements.
2. Efforts as referred to in paragraph (1), are developed and adjusted to the age, level of ability of the child, and their environment so as not to hinder and disturb the child's development.

Article 14

Child protection in the field of Health as referred to in Article 10 letter c, includes:
a. providing facilities and organizing comprehensive health efforts for children, so that every child obtains optimal health status from the womb carried out by the Regional Government;
b. organizing comprehensive and free health efforts for all children including those from poor families;
c. providing health insurance for child victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment;
d. being responsible for maintaining health and caring for children from the womb carried out by families and parents; and
e. endeavoring that children born are protected from diseases that threaten survival and or cause disability carried out by the Regional Government, community, families and parents.

Article 15

The implementation of child protection in the field of education as referred to in Article 10 letter d, includes:
a. providing the widest possible opportunity for children to obtain education conducted by the Regional Government, family and parents;
b. every education provider is prohibited from expelling children from Educational Institutions without guarantees for the continuity of the child's education;
c. implementation of a 12 (twelve) year compulsory education program, regulated in a separate Regional Regulation;
d. providing equal opportunities and accessibility to obtain regular education and special education for children with disabilities; and
e. protecting their rights to obtain education for child victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment.

Article 16

The implementation of child protection in the social field as referred to in Article 10 letter e is conducted in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1\. Making policies on child protection from all forms of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment in all aspects of children's lives;
2\. Making policies on social welfare guarantees for children who experience violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment; and
3\. Conducting training to increase capacity for family welfare consultation institutions.
b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1\. Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families who are victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment;
2\. Providing services for children and families who are victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment including:
1\. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
2\. mediation to related parties;
3\. psychosocial services;
4\. economic assistance and life skills;
5\. temporary shelter;
6\. preparation of social reintegration for child victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment; and
7\. protecting the identity of victims to prevent bad stigma for children.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1\. Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2\. Providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3\. Providing counseling and support from families;
4\. Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services;
5\. Conducting monitoring of the condition of children in long-term alternative care; and
6\. Creating a database on alternative care facilities for children.

Article 17

Child Protection in the field of Special Protection as referred to in Article 10 letter f, includes:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law (ABH);
c. children exploited economically and or sexually;
d. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse (NAPZA);
e. child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking;
f. child victims of violence both physical and/or mental, and children with disabilities; and/or
g. child victims of maltreatment and neglect.

Article 18

1. Children in emergency situations as referred to in Article 17 letter a, consist of:
a. children who become refugees;
b. child victims of riots;
c. child victims of natural disasters; and
d. children in armed conflict situations.
2. The implementation of child protection in emergency situations as referred to in paragraph (1) is conducted in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1\. Making policies on child protection from all forms of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment in emergency situations;
2\. Making policies on fulfillment of basic needs consisting of food, clothing, housing, education, health, learning and recreation, security guarantees, and equal treatment for children who become refugees, victims of violence, victims of natural disasters and children in armed conflict situations; and
3\. Making policies and disaster management mechanisms that take into account the interests of children.
b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1\. Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families who are victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment.
2\. Providing services for children and families who are victims of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment, which include:
a. legal consultation;
b. mediation to related parties;
c. psychosocial services;
d. economic assistance and life skills;
e. safe houses; and
f. shelters.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1\. Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2\. Providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3\. Providing counseling and support from families;
4\. Treating children in emergency situations humanely in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
5\. Providing a sense of security and protection for children who become refugees, victims of violence, victims of natural disasters and children in armed conflict situations; and
6\. Fulfilling special needs for children in emergency situations who experience psychosocial disorders.

Article 19

1. Special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in Article 17 letter b includes children in conflict with the law and child victims of criminal acts, constituting the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government and society.
2. The implementation of special protection for children in conflict with the law as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1\. Making policies regarding education guarantees for children in conflict with the law;
2\. Providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or society, the press, educators, government, law enforcement officials regarding the protection of the identity of children in conflict with the law to avoid negative stigma towards children;
3\. Providing socialization and raising awareness of parents/guardians and/or society, the press, educators, Regional Government and law enforcement officials regarding alternative forms of enforcing discipline without physical and psychological punishment;
4\. Providing socialization to the community regarding understanding restorative justice or justice that restores;
5\. Organizing training to increase the capacity of the community in advocating to law enforcement institutions to make distinctions in handling issues related to children in conflict with the law;
6\. Organizing training to increase the capacity of the community in advocating for the juvenile justice process to provide alternative punishments in the form of measures; and
7\. Creating a complaint mechanism for handling issues related to children in conflict with the law.
b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1\. Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk;
2\. Providing services for children and families such as:
a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation;
c. mediation to related parties;
d. psychosocial services; and
e. protecting the identity of victims.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1\. Providing counseling and the need for family support;
2\. Treatment of children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
3\. Providing guarantees of legal assistance/special assistance for children in conflict with the law;
4\. Providing a sense of security and protection from reporting about the identity of children in conflict with the law to avoid negative stigma towards children;
5\. Providing special facilities and infrastructure for children in conflict with the law;
6\. Providing accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments;
7\. Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services; and
8\. Conducting continuous monitoring and recording of the development of children in conflict with the law.

Article 20

1. Special protection for children who are exploited economically and/or sexually as referred to in Article 17 letter c, constitutes the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government and society.
2. The implementation of special protection for children who are exploited economically and or sexually as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1\. Making policies for the protection of children from all forms of violence, exploitation, and maltreatment in all situations of children's lives;
2\. Making policies for fulfilling special needs for children with disabilities and children experiencing psychosocial disorders who are victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation;
3\. Providing protection guarantees for child victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation;
4\. Providing protection guarantees from all forms of violence, exploitation, neglect and maltreatment for children who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
5\. Providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or society, the press, educators, by the government and law enforcement officials regarding the adverse effects of violence and economic and/or sexual exploitation;
6\. Providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or society, the press, educators, by the government and law enforcement officials regarding the protection of the identity of child victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation; and
7\. Providing socialization regarding the provisions of laws and regulations related to the protection of children who are exploited economically and/or sexually.
b. Risk Reduction Services, which include:
1\. Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk of experiencing economic and/or sexual exploitation;
2\. Providing services for children and families who are victims of violence, and economic and/or sexual exploitation including:
a. legal consultation;
b. legal assistance to victims of sexual violence;
c. mediation to related parties;
d. psychosocial services;
e. economic assistance and life skills;
f. temporary shelter; and
g. protecting the identity of victims.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1\. Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2\. Providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3\. Providing counseling and support from the family;
4\. Treating child victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
5\. Providing a sense of security and protection for child victims of economic and/or sexual exploitation from reporting; and
6\. Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services.

Article 21

1. Supervision, prevention, treatment, and rehabilitation of children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse as referred to in Article 17 letter d and involved in their production and distribution, constitutes the obligation and responsibility of the Regional Government and society.
2. The implementation of protection for children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1\. Making policies for the protection of child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse;
2\. Making special protection policies for children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse; and
3\. Providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or society and children regarding the adverse effects of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse.
b. Risk Reduction Services, which include:
1\. Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk;
2\. Providing services for children and families who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse including:
a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation; and
c. psychosocial services.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1\. Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2\. Providing referrals according to the needs of victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse;
3\. Conducting counseling and support from the family;
4\. Treating child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children; and
5\. Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services.

Article 22

1. Special Protection for child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking as referred to in Article 17 letter e shall be carried out through supervision, protection, prevention, care and rehabilitation efforts by the Regional Government and the community.
2. The implementation of special protection for children who are victims of abduction, sale and trafficking as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1\. Providing protection guarantees for child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking; and
2\. Providing complaint mechanisms and case handling.
b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1\. Conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk; and
2\. Providing services for children and victims of abduction, sale and trafficking which include:
a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation;
c. mediation to related parties;
d. psychosocial services;
e. economic assistance and life skills;
f. temporary shelter; and
g. protecting the identity of victims.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1\. Conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims who are in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2\. Providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3\. Conducting counseling and providing support from the family;
4\. Treating child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
5\. Providing accessibility to obtain information regarding case developments;
6\. Providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services; and
7\. Creating a database of child victims of abduction, sale and trafficking.

Article 23

1. Special Protection for child victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities as referred to in Article 17 letter f shall be carried out through efforts to:
a. disseminate and socialize the provisions of laws and regulations that protect children from becoming victims of violence;
b. obtain equal treatment with other children to achieve full social integration and individual development; and
c. fulfill special needs.
2. The implementation of special protection for children who are victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, which include:
1\. making policies for fulfilling special needs for children with disabilities and children experiencing psychosocial disorders;
2\. providing education guarantees for child victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities;
3\. providing protection guarantees from all forms of violence, exploitation, neglect and abuse for children with disabilities;
4\. providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or the community, the press, educators, by the government and law enforcement officials about the adverse effects of violence;
5\. providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or the community, the press, educators, by the government about protecting the identity of children who are victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities from negative stigma; and
6\. establishing complaint mechanisms for case handling.
b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1\. conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk;
2\. providing services for children and families who are victims of violence which include:
a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation;
c. mediation to related parties;
d. psychosocial services;
e. economic assistance and life skills;
f. temporary shelter; and
g. protecting the identity of victims.
c. Case Handling Services, which include:
1\. conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims who are in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2\. providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3\. conducting counseling and support from the family;
4\. treating children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children;
5\. providing a sense of security and protection from news coverage to avoid negative stigma;
6\. providing special facilities and infrastructure for children with disabilities;
7\. providing rehabilitation and social reintegration services; and
8\. creating a database of child victims of physical and/or mental violence and children with disabilities.

Article 24

1. Protection of child victims of abuse and neglect as referred to in Article 17 letter g shall be carried out through supervision, prevention, care and rehabilitation efforts by the Regional Government and the community.
2. The implementation of protection for children who are victims of abuse and neglect as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in the form of:
a. Prevention Services, including:
1\. providing protection guarantees for child victims of abuse and neglect;
2\. providing protection guarantees from all forms of neglect and abuse for children;
3\. providing socialization and raising awareness of parents and/or the community, the press, educators, the government, law enforcement officials about the adverse effects of abuse and neglect; and
4\. establishing complaint mechanisms for case handling.
b. Risk Reduction Services, including:
1\. conducting early identification, complaint services and assessment of children and families at risk;
2\. providing services for children and families who are victims of neglect and abuse including:
a. guidance and counseling on parenting patterns and behavior change;
b. legal consultation;
c. mediation to related parties;
d. psychosocial services; and
e. temporary shelter.
c. Case Handling Services, including:
1\. conducting immediate rescue efforts for victims who are in dangerous conditions for their safety;
2\. providing referrals according to the needs of victims;
3\. conducting counseling and support from the family;
4\. treating children in a humane manner in accordance with the dignity and rights of children; and
5\. conducting continuous monitoring and recording of the development of child victims of abuse and neglect.
3. The procedures, mechanisms and service standards for special protection for child laborers, child workers and working children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER V

FORMS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN

Article 25

Forms of violence against women and children, include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/orf. other violence.

CHAPTER VI

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 27

Women and children victims of violence receive the following rights:
a. the right to have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. the right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
c. the right to determine their own decisions;
d. the right to obtain information;
e. the right to confidentiality;
f. the right to compensation;
g. the right to social rehabilitation;
h. the right to complaint handling;
i. the right to assistance and legal aid;
j. the right to obtain visum et repertum free of charge; and
k. the right of victims and their families to obtain facilities in the judicial process.

Article 28

Child victims of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 27, are granted special rights, as follows:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma;
e. the right to obtain freedom; and
f. the right to be tried based on the principle of restorative justice.

Article 29

Obligations and responsibilities in the implementation of protection for women and children are the shared responsibility of:
a. Regional Government;
b. village government;
c. Community; and
d. parents, Guardians and/or Family.

Article 30

1. The Regional Government as referred to in Article 29 letter a, is obliged and responsible to:
a. respect, protect and fulfill the rights of victims and the special rights of child victims of violence;
b. prepare and implement programs and activities related to the protection of women and children;
c. implement the protection of women and children from violence;
d. supervise services to victims of violence based on minimum service standards in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
e. allocate budget for the protection of women and children from violence according to the regional financial capacity and budgeted in each fiscal year; and
f. realize a Child-Friendly Regency.
2. In order to prepare and implement effective women and child protection programs as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the Regional Government organizes:
a. a disaggregated gender and child data system; and
b. gender-responsive and child-friendly planning and budgeting.
3. The Regional Government may establish a Regional Technical Implementation Unit for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection to reach services in the Region.

Article 35

1. Prevention as referred to in Article 34 letter a, is implemented by:
a. forming a network in efforts to prevent violence;
b. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
c. establishing a violence prevention system;
d. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children; and
e. providing knowledge about prevention and mechanisms for handling violence against women and children.
2. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented in an integrated manner by Regency Regional Apparatus and related agencies that have principal tasks and functions in the implementation of affairs:
a. women's empowerment and child protection;
b. education;
c. health;
d. social;
e. manpower;
f. youth and sports; and
g. mental and spiritual.
3. In addition to being implemented by Regency Regional Apparatus and related agencies as referred to in paragraph (2), prevention is also implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community, NGOs, community organizations, social organizations and religious organizations; and
c. business sector.

Article 37

Service principles as referred to in Article 36 letter a, consist of:
a. fast;
b. safe and comfortable;
c. sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easily accessible;
f. not charged; and
g. guaranteed confidentiality.

Article 38

Forms of services to victims as referred to in Article 36 letter b, consist of:
a. services to women and children victims of violence, including:
1\. complaint, consultation and counseling services;
2\. assistance services;
3\. health services;
4\. legal aid services; and
5\. repatriation and social reintegration services.
b. special services to women victims of domestic violence or trafficking in persons, including:
1\. complaint services;
2\. pick-up services;
3\. medical and psychological referral services;
4\. shelter services;
5\. assistance and legal aid services; and 6. repatriation and social reintegration services.

Article 40

Assistance services as referred to in Article 38 letter a number 2 include:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;
b. accompanying victims during the psychological recovery process;
c. accompanying victims during the medicolegal process;
d. accompanying victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
e. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
f. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including reporting by mass media;
g. coordinating with other assistants; and
h. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 43

1. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in Article 38 letter a number 5 are carried out to return victims to their families and social environment.
2. Repatriation and social reintegration services as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out by the Regional Government in coordination with:
a. Regency/City Government within one Provincial area or outside the Province; and/or
b. related agencies and institutions both Government and non-Government.
3. Forms of services as referred to in Article 38, are implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the Government/Regional Government and implemented by Regency Regional Apparatus that have tasks and functions in the implementation of affairs:
a. women's empowerment and child protection;
b. social;
c. manpower;
d. health;
e. education;
f. security and order; and
g. mental and spiritual.
4. In carrying out tasks and functions as referred to in paragraph (1) the Regional Government cooperates with the Government, Provincial Government, other Regency/City Governments, and the community.

Article 45

To assist women's empowerment and child protection, the Regional Government may establish:
a. Regional Technical Implementation Unit for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection;
b. Integrated Service Center for Women's Empowerment and Children at the Regional level;
c. Task Force for Protection of Women and Children at the Sub-district Level;
d. Task Unit for Protection of Women and Children at the Village Level;
e. Regional Indonesian Child Protection Commission;
f. Regional Children's Forum; and
g. Other institutions or organizations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 47

1. P2TP2A as referred to in Article 45 letter b, is a forum for women empowerment and child protection services based on the community that functions as an integrated service center in providing protection to women and children.
2. The composition of P2TP2A members as referred to in paragraph (1) among others consists of:
a. elements of the Regional Government;
b. academics;
c. legal experts;
d. psychologists;
e. psychiatrists;
f. religious leaders; and
g. community elements.
3. The main tasks of P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing protection to women and children; and
b. improving the quality of life of women and children.
4. Further provisions regarding the establishment of P2TP2A shall be stipulated by Regent's Decree.

Article 48

1. The Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at Sub-District Level as referred to in Article 45 letter c, is a functional work unit that organizes integrated protection services and handling of cases of violence against children and women at the Sub-District level.
2. The Management of the Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. elements of Sub-District apparatus;
b. agencies or institutions at Sub-District level;
c. elements of Sector Police;
d. elements of Military District Command;
e. community leaders or religious leaders;
f. youth elements; and
g. elements of the Sub-District Family Welfare Development Driving Team.
3. The main tasks of the Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at Sub-District Level as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing services in the context of protecting women and children;
b. receiving community complaints regarding cases of violence against women and children in the Sub-District and Village areas;
c. facilitating women and children who are victims of violence in resolving their problems;
d. socializing to the community about the protection of women and children; and
e. providing assistance for victims or referring to P2TP2A, sector police/resort police, hospitals or other institutions as needed.
4. Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties, and functions of the task force for the protection of women and children at sub-district level as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be stipulated by Sub-District Head's Decree.

Article 49

1. The Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at Village Level as referred to in Article 45 letter d is a functional work unit that organizes integrated protection services and handling of cases of violence against children and women at the Village level.
2. The Management of the Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at Village Level as referred to in paragraph (1) consists of:
a. elements of Village apparatus;
b. elements of Village or Sub-District Supervisory Non-Commissioned Officers;
c. elements of Community Supervisory Non-Commissioned Officers;
d. community leaders or religious leaders;
e. youth elements; and
f. elements of the Village Family Welfare Development Driving Team.
3. The main tasks of the Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at Village Level as referred to in paragraph (1) are:
a. providing services in the context of protecting women and children;
b. receiving community complaints regarding cases of violence against women and children in the Village area;
c. facilitating women and children who are victims of violence in resolving their problems;
d. socializing to the community about the protection of women and children; and
e. providing assistance for victims or referring to the task force for the protection of women and children at Sub-District level.
4. Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties and functions of the task unit for the protection of women and children at village level as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by village head's decree.

Article 51

1. The Regional Indonesian Child Protection Commission as referred to in Article 45 letter e has detailed tasks including:
a. conducting supervision of the implementation of protection and fulfillment of children's rights;
b. providing input and proposals in the formulation of policies regarding the implementation of child protection;
c. collecting data and information regarding child protection;
d. receiving and conducting reviews of community complaints regarding violations of children's rights;
e. conducting mediation on disputes regarding violations of children's rights;
f. conducting cooperation with institutions established by the community in the field of child protection; and
g. other tasks in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
2. In the event of changes to laws and regulations concerning the Regional Indonesian Child Protection Commission, the regulation regarding the Regional Indonesian Child Protection Commission shall be guided by laws and regulations which are further regulated by Regent's Regulation.

CHAPTER X

CHILD-FRIENDLY REGENCY

Article 56

1. The Regional Government is obliged to implement a Child-Friendly Regency implementation system in order to fulfill children's rights. (2) The development of a Child-Friendly Regency is carried out based on the following principles:
a. good governance, namely transparency, accountability, participation, information openness, and rule of law;
b. non-discrimination, namely not distinguishing ethnicity, race, religion, gender, language, political views, national origin, economic status, physical or psychological condition of the child, or other factors;
c. the best interests of the child, namely making what is best for the child the primary consideration in every policy, program, and activity;
d. the right to life, survival, and development of the child, namely guaranteeing the right to life, survival and development of the child to the maximum extent possible; and
e. respect for the views of the child, namely recognizing and ensuring that every child who has the ability to express their opinion is given the opportunity to express their views freely on all matters affecting them.
2. The implementation instruments of the Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. development system and public services;
b. Child-Friendly Health Services;
c. Child-Friendly Family;
d. Child-Friendly School; and
e. Child-Friendly Environment;
f. Child-Friendly Islamic Boarding School.
3. The Regional Government in implementing the Child-Friendly Regency as referred to in paragraph (1) must pay attention to the Child-Friendly Regency Indicators which include:
a. institutional; and
b. children's rights clusters.
4. Institutional strengthening as referred to in paragraph (4) letter a includes:
a. the existence of laws and regulations and policies for the fulfillment of children's rights;
b. percentage of budget for the fulfillment of children's rights, including budget for institutional strengthening;
c. number of laws and regulations, policies, programs and activities that receive input from Children's Forums and other children's groups;
d. availability of Human Resources (HR) trained in the Convention on the Rights of the Child (CRC) and capable of applying children's rights into policies, programs, and activities;
e. availability of disaggregated child data by gender, age, and sub-district;
f. involvement of community institutions in the fulfillment of children's rights; and
g. involvement of the business sector in the fulfillment of children's rights.

Article 57

Children's rights clusters as referred to in Article 56 paragraph (4) letter b which include:
a. Civil rights and freedoms which include:
1\. children's right to identity;
2\. right to protection of identity;
3\. right to express and voice opinions;
4\. right to think, have conscience, religion, and belief;
5\. right to organize and assemble peacefully;
6\. right to protection of private life;
7\. right to access appropriate information; and
8\. right to be free from torture and other cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment or punishment.
b. Rights to family environment and care which include:
1\. parental guidance and responsibility;
2\. children separated from parents;
3\. reunification;
4\. illegal transfer of children;
5\. welfare support;
6\. children forced to be separated from the family environment;
7\. child adoption;
8\. periodic review of placement; and
9\. violence and neglect.
c. Children's rights to basic health and welfare which include:
a. children with disabilities receive access to health services and welfare;
b. health and health services;
c. social security health services and facilities; and
d. standard of living;
d. Children's rights to education, leisure time utilization and cultural activities which include:
1. education;
2. objectives of education; and
3. holiday activities, cultural activities, and sports.
e. Protection for children in special protection which includes:
1\. children in emergency situations;
2\. children in conflict with the law;
3\. children in exploitation situations; and
4\. children belonging to minority and isolated groups.

Article 58

1. To make the development of a Child-Friendly Regency more effective, a Child-Friendly Regency Task Force is formed which is established by Regent's Decree.
2. The membership of the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) may come from the Regional Government, children's representatives, business sector, religious leaders, community leaders and other parties as needed.
3. In carrying out their main duties, members of the Child-Friendly Regency Task Force as referred to in paragraph (2) have detailed tasks including:
a. coordinating various efforts to develop a Child-Friendly Regency;
b. preparing a Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly Regency Development;
c. carrying out socialization, advocacy and communication for the development of a Child-Friendly Regency;
d. monitoring the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly Regency Development;
e. conducting evaluations at the end of each year on the implementation of policies, programs and activities in the Regional Action Plan for Child-Friendly Regency Development; and
f. submitting reports to the Regent.

Article 62

1. Reporting on the implementation of women's empowerment and child protection shall be conducted by:
a. P2TP2A to the Regent;
b. Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children at Sub-district Level to the Sub-district Head;
c. Task Unit for the Protection of Women and Children at Village Level to the Village Head; and
d. Regional Indonesian Child Protection Commission to the Regent through the Head of Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.
2. Reporting as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be submitted in writing, covering:
a. administration;
b. finance;
c. services; and
d. performance.
3. Submission of written reports as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented at least once every 6 (six) months.
